so grip strength is extremely important  for your overall performance and even  your health it's been linked to  mortality rates but here's something  that perhaps you didn't know about grip  strength that much of it come from your  little finger it's logical and  understandable to assume that when it  comes to grip strength vigor must be  better that the index finger and the  thumb must contribute the massive  proportion of grip strength and that  your little finger probably doesn't  really do much in fact though this  couldn't be further from the truth by  gripping onto a rope as though you're  going to climb it and use just the top  two fingers you'll find that it's really  hard to get a stable enough grip to  really hold on now try through the same  thing with the little finger and the  thumb and you'll find you have a much  more secure grip one study found that  when measuring grip strength via a hand  dynamometer the ulnar digits were  responsible for 34 to 67 percent of grip  strength with the mean average being 55%  this is something was actually well  known by the East Samurai and apparently  if the samurai betrayed their master  what would then happen as punishment is  that they'd have their little finger cut  off this might seem odd with the samurai  knew that by removing the little finger  they'd be able to very effectively  impair the samurais ability to fight  with a sword it'll be much easier to  knock it out of their hands with a  well-timed blow so why is this well the  owners digits are controlled by a  separate muscle called the flexor carpi  ulnaris and the ulnar nerve but that  power and your little finger isn't  available in an isolation just using  your little finger you won't be able to  exert that much strength and that's  because the little finger gets most of  its power by working in tandem with the  other fingers the second fingers we've  discussed and also the thumb think about  it we call them opposing thumbs what do  they oppose well the most opposite  2-digit is the little finger and by  clenching them together we're able to  make a nice closed circle remove either  one of those and you have a weak link if  you try performing pull-ups with your  little fingers sticking up in the air  you're going to find that a lot more  difficult now try doing the same thing  with your index finger sticking up in  the air and you'll find it's actually  not as difficult another interesting bit  of information is that the strength that  the little finger contributes is  disproportionately greater on the  dominant hand than it is on the  non-dominant hand what's going on there  though is anybody's guess so what do we  do with all this information well the  obvious thing to consider is just that  we need to train our little fingers and  it's actually very easy to overlook this  if you're using a grip trainer for  instance and squeezing you might find  that the angle of  grip train it makes it easy it's a  little finger to play a much smaller  role so make sure you're not doing that  and consciously make sure to contract  that little finger likewise if you're  using fat gripz or just squeezing the  bow was performing other movements  really involve the little finger really  make sure that it too is playing a part  it also tells us it's really not too  good to injure the little finger so look  after it make sure you don't bend it  backwards when playing sports and if you  do give it ample time to recover and  don't lift anything too heavy over your  head whilst you're in that condition  you're much more likely to drop it on  your face what's more is that you should  really think about the position of your  grip when training meaning the way that  you use your thumb and fingers together  in particular wrapping your thumb back  around and over your fingers when  lifting weights or hanging from rings or  a bar will help you bring your wrist and  pinky into a stronger position simply  changing the position of a single finger  moving the knuckle of your pinky finger  on top of the bar during a pull-up for  instance you can meanwhile alter the  muscles primarily being worked in this  case you're adding flexion to the wrist  and you're increasing your lats  activation likewise when you train the  farmers walk try bringing the pinky  knuckle under the handle of the weight  to again increase flexion in the wrist  this will train the grip more  effectively than if you give no thought  to the position of the fingers and the  wrists training wrist flexion this way  is actually incredibly valuable when it  comes to preventing injury but make sure  you're training with both a supplemented  and a pronated grip as well as a neutral  grip to avoid imbalances which can  actually make things worse and if you  build this up you'll find that things  like rope climbs and muscle ups become  much easier and far less likely to snap  your arms off so you go guys that was  just a small little tip I hope found it  useful and interesting if you did then  please leave like please comment down  below helps me out immensely do you have  any quick tips to share about grip I've  got much more on the way of course more  quick tips like this one lots more  in-depth videos looking at things like  va2 max and lung capacity coming very  soon balance training polymath series on  the way so for that sounds good then  thanks a ton for watching and I'll see  you next time bye for now  [Music]
 

